<.guide_page
  title={@guide_meta.title}
  dek={@guide_meta.dek}
  date={@guide_meta.date}
  read_time={@guide_meta.read_time}
>
  <p>
    We tested an uptime read and a Caddy reload through emisar, using a real service that returned
    a line of text. The read ran immediately. The reload waited for a person. After approval,
    we checked both the runner result and the HTTP response.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our first attempt failed. That failure is part of the example: an approval gave the request
    permission to proceed, but the host still refused it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="setup">Use a service whose response you can check</h2>
  <p>
    Use a disposable Linux host with Caddy and the emisar runner. Install the
    <code>linux-core</code>
    and <code>caddy</code>
    packs, then connect your MCP client to the same
    workspace. The <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}>quickstart</.link>
    covers that setup.
    Keep the runner configuration and state separate from the service files.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start Caddy with this configuration in <code>/opt/caddy-demo/before.Caddyfile</code>.
    Keep its admin endpoint on localhost and choose a free HTTP port:
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Initial test configuration">&#123;
  admin localhost:2019
  auto_https off
&#125;
:8080 &#123;
  respond "before approval"
&#125;</.docs_code>
  <p>
    Copy it to <code>/opt/caddy-demo/Caddyfile</code>
    and change that copy's response to <code>after approval</code>. Leave Caddy running on the initial file. Fetch its HTTP URL:
    it should still say <code>before approval</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    We kept the default policy: low-risk actions run immediately; high-risk actions need approval.
    Our runner admitted only <code>linux.uptime</code> and <code>caddy.reload_config</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="read">Let the diagnostic run</h2>
  <p>
    Use <code>list_runners</code>
    and <code>list_packs</code>
    to discover your test host and its
    exact references. Inspect <code>linux.uptime</code>
    with <code>get_action</code>, then call <code>run_action</code>
    with those references and an empty argument object.
  </p>
  <p>
    In our test, the read finished with <.status_badge status="success" />
    and exit code <code>0</code>, returning uptime and load averages. It needed no approval.
    In our Docker container, uptime describes the Docker VM's kernel.
  </p>

  <h2 id="request">The reload waits</h2>
  <p>
    Inspect <code>caddy.reload_config</code>, then request it for the test host with this argument:
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Action arguments">&#123;"file": "/opt/caddy-demo/Caddyfile"&#125;</.docs_code>
  <p>
    Explain the intended response change in the request's reason. The response includes
    <.status_badge status="pending_approval" />, the run ID, a continuation and a link to the decision.
    Before approving, fetch the HTTP URL again. Our service still said <code>before approval</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="host-check">Why our first approved request was refused</h2>
  <p>
    We initially put the Caddyfile beside the runner configuration under <code>/srv</code>.
    A person approved the request, but the runner refused to let an action read a file inside its
    own configuration directory:
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Actual runner refusal">argument file: path /srv/Caddyfile is inside the runner's own configuration or state</.docs_code>
  <p>
    The run ended as <.status_badge status="validation_failed" /> before Caddy ran.
    The HTTP response stayed <code>before approval</code>.
  </p>
  <.docs_screenshot
    src="/images/docs/caddy-demo/runner-refusal.png"
    alt="An approved Caddy reload marked validation failed because its file was inside the runner configuration directory."
    title="Approved request, refused on the host"
    caption="The runner refused the first request before executing Caddy."
    preview_h="max-h-80"
  />
  <p>
    We moved only the test Caddyfile to <code>/opt/caddy-demo/Caddyfile</code> and validated it
    with Caddy. The policy and runner protection stayed in place. The corrected request needed
    its own approval.
  </p>

  <h2 id="result">Then the service changed</h2>
  <p>
    A person reviewed and approved the corrected request. We followed its
    <code>wait_for_run</code>
    continuation until the runner returned <.status_badge status="success" />
    and exit code <code>0</code>.
    An independent HTTP check then returned <code>after approval</code>.
  </p>
  <.docs_screenshot
    src="/images/docs/caddy-demo/approved-reload.png"
    alt="The corrected Caddy reload marked success, with its executed command, approval and recorded output."
    title="Corrected reload completed"
    caption="The successful run and the separate HTTP check agreed on the result."
    preview_h="max-h-80"
  />
  <p>
    Caddy printed configuration-environment and file-format warnings. They are preserved in the
    output record. The command succeeded and the service returned the new response.
  </p>

  <h2 id="repeat">Repeat the test</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/images/docs/caddy-demo/method.md">reproduction steps</a>
    include the setup,
    tool inputs and checks. The <a href="/images/docs/caddy-demo/evidence.json">evidence JSON</a>
    includes the exact versions, policy, calls, warnings and runner journal, including the failed attempt.
  </p>
  <p>
    We used scripted MCP bridge calls, a local development control plane and a real Caddy 2.11.4
    service in a Linux container on macOS. Both approvals were single-use. Standing grants and
    revocation are covered in <.link navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}>Policies and approvals</.link>.
  </p>
</.guide_page>
